            <ArticleTitle>Predictors for diagnosing metabolic syndrome among hypertensive patients in a tertiary care centre.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>132-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.dsx.2015.02.011</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1871-4021(15)00024-7</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Metabolic syndrome (MS) was present in 71% of adult hypertensive subjects according to modified National Cholesterol Education Program Adult Treatment Panel III (NCEP ATP-III) criteria in a tertiary care health centre. Female gender and higher body mass index (BMI) were predictors for MS among these patients. BMI cut off of 23 (overweight) had sensitivity of 94% and positive predictive value of 75% for diagnosing MS among them.</AbstractText>
